''trans.'' '''1.''' To open the folds of; expand; spread out; change from a folded condition, in any sense of the word ''fold''. ''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 1702; ''Pope'', Iliad, ii. 978.—'''2.''' To lay open to view or contemplation; make known in all the details; develop; disclose; reveal: as, to ''unfold'' one’s designs; to ''unfold'' the principles of a science.

{{smaller block|<poem>The Holy Fader wondred on that he told,
Off tho merueles that ther gan ''vnfold''.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 5124.}}
Time shall ''unfold'' what plaited cunning hides.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 1. 283.}}}}

'''3.''' To show, or let be seen; display.

{{smaller block|[Lightning] that in a spleen ''unfolds'' both heaven and earth. {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., i. 1. 146.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become opened out; be spread apart; become disclosed or developed; develop itself.

{{smaller block|I see thy beauty gradually ''unfold''.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Eleänore.}}}}

